WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.955 --> 00:00:03.330
PRIYANKA VERGADIA:
Hey, everyone.

00:00:03.330 --> 00:00:04.130
How are you?

00:00:04.130 --> 00:00:06.476
Are you excited
about this episode?

00:00:06.476 --> 00:00:09.878
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:15.230 --> 00:00:19.010
Welcome to AI Adventures, where
we explore the art, science,

00:00:19.010 --> 00:00:20.750
and tools of machine learning.

00:00:20.750 --> 00:00:23.400
I am Priyanka Vergadia
and in this episode,

00:00:23.400 --> 00:00:25.430
I'm going to show
you a tool that

00:00:25.430 --> 00:00:29.120
enables you to automatically
translate your video and audio

00:00:29.120 --> 00:00:32.060
content to text in real time.

00:00:32.060 --> 00:00:34.760
World has become rather
small with technology.

00:00:34.760 --> 00:00:37.700
We are all customers of
services that are made

00:00:37.700 --> 00:00:39.540
in another part of the world.

00:00:39.540 --> 00:00:43.070
So we experience situations
where the audio or video

00:00:43.070 --> 00:00:45.170
is in a language
that we don't speak,

00:00:45.170 --> 00:00:47.780
and as a developer
of such global apps,

00:00:47.780 --> 00:00:50.630
we clearly need to overcome
the language barrier,

00:00:50.630 --> 00:00:52.910
and it's not as
simple as it seems.

00:00:52.910 --> 00:00:55.970
But what's so
complicated about it?

00:00:55.970 --> 00:00:59.180
It's the data and the
files, because they are not

00:00:59.180 --> 00:01:00.830
in the form of text.

00:01:00.830 --> 00:01:04.099
We have to first convert
original data into text

00:01:04.099 --> 00:01:07.310
by using speech to text
to get a transcription.

00:01:07.310 --> 00:01:09.410
Then we need to feed
those transcriptions

00:01:09.410 --> 00:01:13.970
into the Translation API
to get the translated text.

00:01:13.970 --> 00:01:17.240
These are a lot of steps,
which cause a lot of friction

00:01:17.240 --> 00:01:19.340
and add latency along the way.

00:01:19.340 --> 00:01:22.670
And it is much harder to get
a good quality translation

00:01:22.670 --> 00:01:26.570
because you might lose context
while splitting sentences.

00:01:26.570 --> 00:01:28.670
So you eventually
make a trade off

00:01:28.670 --> 00:01:32.660
between quality, speed,
and ease of deployment.

00:01:32.660 --> 00:01:35.900
That's where Media
Translation API comes in.

00:01:35.900 --> 00:01:38.810
It simplifies this process
by abstracting away

00:01:38.810 --> 00:01:42.260
the transcription and
translation behind a single API

00:01:42.260 --> 00:01:45.260
call, allowing you to
get real-time translation

00:01:45.260 --> 00:01:49.320
from video and audio data with
high translation accuracy.

00:01:49.320 --> 00:01:51.680
There are a large number
of use cases for this.

00:01:51.680 --> 00:01:54.470
You can enable real-time
engagement for users

00:01:54.470 --> 00:01:56.930
by streaming translations
from a microphone

00:01:56.930 --> 00:01:58.970
or pre-recorded audio file.

00:01:58.970 --> 00:02:00.920
You can power an
interactive experience

00:02:00.920 --> 00:02:04.640
on your platform with video
chat with translated captions

00:02:04.640 --> 00:02:09.020
or add subtitles to your videos
real time as they are played.

00:02:09.020 --> 00:02:11.580
Now let's see how it works.

00:02:11.580 --> 00:02:13.160
I'm going to say
a few things here

00:02:13.160 --> 00:02:16.190
and we will see how Media
Translation API translates

00:02:16.190 --> 00:02:19.130
the microphone input into text.

00:02:19.130 --> 00:02:21.650
"New Delhi is the
capital of India.

00:02:21.650 --> 00:02:25.640
Washington DC is the capital
of the United States."

00:02:25.640 --> 00:02:28.070
Here we see that
the API is trying

00:02:28.070 --> 00:02:30.290
to understand the input
from the microphone

00:02:30.290 --> 00:02:33.440
and giving us the partial
and the final translation

00:02:33.440 --> 00:02:35.530
in real time.

00:02:35.530 --> 00:02:37.290
Now how did I set this up?

00:02:37.290 --> 00:02:39.120
Let me show you.

00:02:39.120 --> 00:02:42.510
In our project, we enable
Media Translation API

00:02:42.510 --> 00:02:46.320
and get the service account
and key for authentication.

00:02:46.320 --> 00:02:49.440
Get the Python code sample
from GitHub, and in the Media

00:02:49.440 --> 00:02:53.670
Translation folder, we see
the files translate from file

00:02:53.670 --> 00:02:55.230
and translate from mic.

00:02:55.230 --> 00:02:58.140
First, we create a Python
virtual environment

00:02:58.140 --> 00:03:02.100
and install our dependencies
from requirements.text.

00:03:02.100 --> 00:03:05.400
Now if we examine or
translate from mic file,

00:03:05.400 --> 00:03:08.490
we see that we are creating
a client for the Media

00:03:08.490 --> 00:03:10.620
Translation API service.

00:03:10.620 --> 00:03:13.740
This client includes the
audio encoding, the language

00:03:13.740 --> 00:03:15.840
we will speak into
the microphone,

00:03:15.840 --> 00:03:18.780
and the language we want
our translations to be in.

00:03:18.780 --> 00:03:22.140
Then with that client, we stream
the audio from our microphone

00:03:22.140 --> 00:03:25.050
up to the Cloud and start
listening to the responses

00:03:25.050 --> 00:03:27.420
from the service,
and as we listen,

00:03:27.420 --> 00:03:30.240
we are printing those
responses as an output.

00:03:30.240 --> 00:03:33.510
This is also where you would
incorporate this output

00:03:33.510 --> 00:03:35.620
into your application.

00:03:35.620 --> 00:03:38.790
Now, while we are here, let's
change our source language

00:03:38.790 --> 00:03:43.380
to Hindi and target language
to English, and try this again.

00:03:43.380 --> 00:03:44.010
Now let's say.

00:03:44.010 --> 00:03:50.600
[SPEAKING HINDI]

00:03:50.600 --> 00:03:53.690
And it rightly says,
"Hi, how are you today?

00:03:53.690 --> 00:03:55.460
Weather is very nice today.

00:03:55.460 --> 00:03:57.335
We should go out for a walk."

00:03:57.335 --> 00:03:58.670
Decent, right?

00:03:58.670 --> 00:04:02.540
In this episode, we learned how
Media Translation API delivers

00:04:02.540 --> 00:04:06.740
high quality real-time speech
translation to our applications

00:04:06.740 --> 00:04:08.750
directly from our microphone.

00:04:08.750 --> 00:04:11.450
You can even provide an
input audio file and achieve

00:04:11.450 --> 00:04:12.900
a similar output.

00:04:12.900 --> 00:04:14.630
There's a sample
in the same folder.

00:04:14.630 --> 00:04:16.550
Try it out yourself
and let me know how

00:04:16.550 --> 00:04:19.130
it goes in the comments below.

00:04:19.130 --> 00:04:27.500
[SPEAKING HINDI] like or
subscribe [SPEAKING HINDI]..

